From e5598ad7138e2746a2451a79a86fe35150c3b9b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 06:23:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge, windowing/gbm: what the converted GUI level
 saves now that single-layer pictures skip the composer

With the decoder's planes taken as the composer's, an HDR10+ frame costs
less in the source transport, and so does one with GUI elements over it.
Measured again on Mesa's software OpenGL ES at 3840x2160 with Kodi's
composite: without GUI elements 35.1 ms; with a band of subtitles 71.9 in
the source transport and 73.4 converted (nothing saved: the measured level
is left at once, as CDVGuiLevel leaves a level that saves nothing); with
the player information over half of the picture 105.1 and 83.1 (21 %
less). A profile 7 picture drawn without its layer, whose mapping is not
the identity, still saves 18 % with the band (83.0 and 68.3). Comments
only: the numbers where the decision is described.
---
 tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_render.c | 6 +++++-
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/DVGuiLevel.h  | 4 +++-
 2 files changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_render.c b/tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_render.c
index e4c7fb3..333c029 100644
--- a/tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_render.c
+++ b/tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_render.c
@@ -1147,7 +1147,11 @@ static bool source_possible(struct dvbridge_renderer *r, const struct pl_frame *
  * single layer with a band of GUI over 14 % of the raster 91.0 ms a frame in the source transport,
  * 72.9 converted (zink 107.7 and 88.5), with a panel over half of it 124.6 and 82.5 (147.5 and
  * 98.4); without GUI elements 53.5 in the source transport and 69.2 converted, which is why only
- * the frames with GUI elements go so. What it changes, on ten pictures of an HDR10+ stream against
+ * the frames with GUI elements go so. Hades: with the decoder's planes taken as the composer's
+ * (identity_planes()) those HDR10+ frames cost less in the source transport: the band 71.9 ms
+ * against 73.4 converted (nothing saved, and the caller's measured level leaves it: CDVGuiLevel's
+ * NO_GAIN), the panel 105.1 against 83.1, without GUI elements 35.1; a profile 7 picture drawn
+ * without its layer (its mapping is not the identity) 83.0 against 68.3 with the band. What it changes, on ten pictures of an HDR10+ stream against
  * the decoded picture: the luma of 99.95 % of the pixels the same, 0.045 % one code of 4095 apart,
  * 0.0015 % more (36 at most, colours that BT.2020 RGB clamps), and the chroma of the rows it is
  * sited on the same but in 49 of 41 million samples (3 codes at most); the rows between them
diff --git a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/DVGuiLevel.h b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/DVGuiLevel.h
index eb30e36..f627f6d 100644
--- a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/DVGuiLevel.h
+++ b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/DVGuiLevel.h
@@ -43,7 +43,9 @@ namespace GBM
  * converted back), the frames without GUI elements less: only the frames with GUI elements go so,
  * and only where they are measured not to keep up, as here. What it changes and saves, measured: see
  * gui_converts() in dvbridge_render.c (on ten HDR10+ pictures the luma the same in 99.95 % of the
- * pixels; with a panel over half of the raster 34 % less time on a software renderer). A picture
+ * pixels; on a software renderer, with the player information over half of an HDR10+ picture 21 %
+ * less time, with subtitles over a profile 7 picture drawn without its layer 18 % less, and with
+ * subtitles over an HDR10+ picture nothing, which NO_GAIN then leaves). A picture
  * with the residual drawn steps those frames down its ladder instead (CDVGuiStep).
  *
  * The frames measured are those with GUI elements whose picture the bridge can draw above level 0
-- 
2.53.0

